   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Without synchronization, the CPU issues step <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i + 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7429em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span> while the GPU runs step <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6595em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span></span></span></span>, so a step costs <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max(2, 6) = 6</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">max</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span></span></span></span> ms. With <code>.item()</code> every step, the CPU waits for the GPU, then the GPU waits while the CPU issues the next step: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 + 6 = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">6</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span> ms. The loss is the same; only the overlap is lost. When the CPU and GPU times are close, synchronizing every step nearly doubles the time.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept1",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is the arithmetic intensity of a kernel, and how does the roofline use it?",
  "answer": "FLOPs divided by bytes moved to and from memory; attainable throughput is the smaller of peak compute and bandwidth times intensity, so kernels below the ridge point are memory-bound.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept2",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why is an n by n by n matrix multiplication compute-bound for large n while an elementwise addition is always memory-bound?",
  "answer": "The matmul does 2n^3 FLOPs on 3n^2 values, so its intensity grows like n; the addition does one FLOP per three values moved, whatever the size.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept3",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does calling .item() on a GPU loss every step slow training?",
  "answer": "It forces the CPU to wait until the GPU has finished all queued work, so the CPU can no longer issue the next step's kernels while the GPU computes, and the two stop overlapping.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept4",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How does tiling raise the arithmetic intensity of a matrix multiplication?",
  "answer": "Each block loads a tile of A and B into fast on-chip memory and reuses every loaded value tile times, so traffic to global memory falls by the tile size.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept5",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is kernel fusion and why does it help memory-bound operations?",
  "answer": "Combining several elementwise steps into one kernel keeps intermediate values in registers instead of writing them to memory and reading them back, which cuts the bytes moved.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L11/concept6",
  "lesson": "L11",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Roughly how many FLOPs does one training step cost per token for a model with N parameters?",
  "answer": "About 6N: 2N for the forward pass and 4N for the backward pass, which computes gradients with respect to both inputs and weights.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
